Condition
---------

lab-process-maild runs lab-process-mail and stops at the first run
that exits non-zero, as posted in "[PATCH v2 0/2] lab-process-mail:
Add lab-process-maild" [1]. Every failure therefore stops the inbox
until someone notices and runs lab-process-maild start again.

Only one of those failures tells anyone. When the agent pass leaves
its message in the inbox, lab-process-mail posts "stuck: <subject>"
to ntfy and exits 1. Every other failure exits non-zero in silence:

- The fetch at the start of the run fails, for example during a
  network outage (jj git fetch, src/lab-process-mail/lab-process-mail
  line 62).
- A rebase in get_up_to_date fails.
- claude itself exits non-zero, for example on an expired login or a
  usage limit. The pass runs in a subshell under set -e (line 135),
  so the script exits with claude's status before it reaches the
  inbox check and its notice. This is from reading the code; I have
  not seen it happen. A quick check confirms the sh behavior:

      $ sh -c 'set -e; (false); echo after'; echo $?
      1

Expected: a transient failure costs one interval and the daemon
carries on; a stuck message is reported and stops the work only as
far as it has to. Actual: both stop the daemon, and only the stuck
case sends a notice.

Diagnosis
---------

lab-process-mail gives every failure the same exit status, so the
daemon cannot tell a message the agent could not answer from a run
that never reached the agent. Stopping on every failure was the safe
choice for the stuck case, where retrying spends an agent pass on the
same message every interval. It is the wrong choice for a transient
failure, where retrying is free and would succeed.

The notice is tied to the one path that checks the inbox, not to the
exit, so every other path is silent.

Possible fixes
--------------

1. Distinct exit statuses. lab-process-mail exits 0 when it answered
   a message or the inbox was empty, 2 when the message is stuck, and
   1 for anything else. lab-process-maild stops on 2, and on 1 logs,
   sleeps the interval, and tries again. Cost: a failure that is not
   transient, such as a broken jj config, retries every minute, but
   those runs end before the agent starts, so they cost a fetch, not
   a pass. Small change, to both scripts and their tests.

2. Notify on every failure. An EXIT trap in lab-process-mail posts
   "failed: <status> (inbox N)" whenever it exits non-zero without
   having sent the stuck notice. Cost: during an outage that lasts an
   hour, combined with fix 1, that is sixty notices; the daemon could
   instead notify only on the first failure after a success. Small
   change.

3. Skip a stuck message instead of stopping. lab-process-mail tags
   the stuck message, say tag:stuck, and oldest_inbox_message skips
   tag:stuck, so the rest of the inbox keeps moving while the stuck
   message waits for you. Removing the tag retries it. Cost: a stuck
   message is easier to overlook, and a pass that is stuck for a
   reason that affects every message, such as a broken prompt,
   would spend a pass on each one before anyone noticed. That case
   is covered by stopping after a few stuck messages in a row. This
   needs the tag to survive notmuch new, which I have not checked
   against how the list's tags are derived.

4. Restart under daemon(8) with -r or -R. No code, but it retries the
   stuck message every interval too, which is the cost the current
   design avoids.

I recommend 1 and 2 together: the daemon survives an outage, the stuck
case still stops it, and every failure tells you. 3 is worth doing
once a stuck message has actually held up the inbox. I can send 1 and
2 as a patch on top of the lab-process-maild series if you agree.

